+### To-device messaging operations (story to-device-messaging, epic e2ee-client-support; migration 000059)
+
+`PUT /_matrix/client/v3/sendToDevice/{eventType}/{txnId}` and `to_device.events` of `/sync` queue opaque message contents per (user, device) in the tables `to_device_messages` and `to_device_txns`. Nothing to configure: the migration creates the tables, the runtime role gets its grants there. Operator facts: (1) the queue is database state (every node answers the same, a restart or a rolling deploy loses no message; a message that was delivered but not yet acknowledged by the next `since` token is delivered again); (2) the Oban cron job `Nebu.Workers.ToDevicePurgeWorker` (hourly, queue `oauth_cleanup`) deletes messages older than 7 days and idempotency records older than 24 hours; delivery never returns an expired message, so a stopped job only keeps rows longer; (3) a device removal (`DELETE /devices`, `/delete_devices`, logout, logout-all, the Admin UI revoke) deletes the queue and the records of the device, so a mass logout (for example the maintenance procedure above) discards every queued message and clients simply retry; (4) the per-user rate limit of the send (60 per minute, burst 30) is node-local (it lives in the memory of each node): with N nodes the effective limit is up to N x the documented limit; (5) the GDPR sweep above deletes the queue, the records and the messages SENT by erased users; the same statement for one user is `DELETE FROM to_device_messages WHERE sender IN (...)` (the messages are queued in other users' devices). The limits (500 messages and 8 MiB per device, 2000 messages and 32 MiB per user, 100 messages per sender and device, 100 targets per request) are application settings `:nebu, :to_device_*` (defaults in 08-concepts "To-device messaging"); a full queue evicts its oldest messages and logs the number only.
