diff --git a/docs/architecture/08-concepts.md b/docs/architecture/08-concepts.md
index 722a7bc9..a30961ba 100644
--- a/docs/architecture/08-concepts.md
+++ b/docs/architecture/08-concepts.md
@@ -1403,13 +1403,41 @@ applies when the event grows, so a tag can still be deleted after an operator lo
 - *Logging:* the route normalizer replaces the client-chosen room and session id segments of `room_keys/keys/**`.
 - *Erasure:* both tables are in `UserDeletion.erased_tables/0` (sessions first, the soft-delete markers too); the creating writers (POST /version, PUT /keys) take the `sync_global` lock and refuse a user whose erasure is under way (`403 M_USER_DEACTIVATED`).
 
+### To-device messaging (story to-device-messaging)
+
+`PUT /_matrix/client/v3/sendToDevice/{eventType}/{txnId}` and the `to_device` member of `GET /sync` (Matrix CS v1.18 "Send-to-Device messaging") are an OPAQUE per-device queue: the content of a message is never decrypted, interpreted or logged (the `RequestLogger` captures no body of the route and redacts the content of sync responses; the `RouteNormalizer` replaces the event type and the txn id of the path; a failing statement logs its error kind only). ADR-011 stays: this enables no E2EE room, `m.room.encryption` is still rejected. SQL is inline in the handler (ADR-018); the state lives only in `to_device_messages` (the queue; `user_id` is the recipient, `sender`, `device_id`, `created_at`) and `to_device_txns` (the idempotency records: `user_id` is the sender, `device_id` the sending device, `event_type`, `txn_id`) (migration 000059, runtime-role grants, row level security enabled and forced) in `Nebu.Matrix.ToDeviceStore`, so the queue, the idempotency and the acknowledgement position are the same on every node and survive a restart (ADR-020).
+
+*Errors.* `400 M_NOT_JSON` (malformed JSON), `400 M_BAD_JSON` (`messages` missing or not an object, a user or device entry that is not an object, a body that is not an object), `400 M_INVALID_PARAM` (an event type outside `^[a-zA-Z0-9._-]{1,255}$`, a txn id of 0 or more than 255 bytes), `413 M_TOO_LARGE` (a body above 64 KiB, more than 50 recipient entries; the request is refused whole, never truncated, and its txn id is not burnt), `429 M_LIMIT_EXCEEDED` with `retry_after_ms` (the sender's rate limit, a transaction that is in flight, a sender whose erasure holds its lock), `403 M_USER_DEACTIVATED` (a sender whose erasure is under way), `500 M_UNKNOWN` (generic text). Everything about WHO can be reached is a silent `200 {}`.
+
+*Order of a request.* Authentication, then the per-user rate limit, and only THEN the body is read (the body is read only after the authentication, with a 64 KiB cap; `JsonBody` leaves the body of the route unfetched): an anonymous or junk-credential request is 401 without its body being read, an exhausted bucket is 429 before the body is parsed or validated (a refused request still costs its token). The limit is 60 per minute, burst 30, per USER (own tier `to_device`, not per IP, a `*` request costs one token); the buckets are node-local, so in a cluster the effective limit is up to N x the documented limit.
+
+*Limits and budgets (every per-item cap has an aggregate cap).* Per request: 64 KiB body, 50 recipient entries (`*` counts as one entry; an unknown or unreachable recipient counts too), at most 100 targets after the `*` expansion (silently truncated, deterministic). Per recipient device: 500 messages and 8 MiB. Per recipient user: 2000 messages and 32 MiB over all devices. Per sender and recipient device: 100 messages, per sender and recipient user: 200 messages and 4 MiB (one sender cannot fill another user's queue; beyond its share the sender's own oldest go first). The byte budgets count the canonical content bytes plus 128 per row. Per sending device: 1000 idempotency records (the oldest is forgotten). A sync response carries at most 100 messages and 1 MiB (the first message is always carried); the rest comes with the next sync without waiting for `timeout`. Messages are kept 7 days (the TTL is decided by the delivery itself, the purge job is housekeeping), idempotency records 24 hours. All are application settings `:nebu, :to_device_<name>` read at request time (`ToDeviceStore.limits/0`).
+
+*A full queue evicts the oldest.* When a budget is exceeded the OLDEST messages of that device (or of the sender share) are evicted; at the USER level the eviction is fair: the oldest message of the sender with the largest share goes first, so a flooding co-member never evicts the older messages of a sender below its share; the sender is never told (the same `200 {}`, no 429: a 429 would reveal the recipient's state and let one sender's flood block the others), and the count is logged without content.
+
+*Recipients and no oracle.* Recipients are the sender's own devices (no room needed) and the devices of users who share a room with the sender where BOTH sides are `join` (evaluated at send time, not at delivery); an invitation opens no send path (it needs no consent, and in-room verification does not use to-device messages). Every other recipient (no shared joined room, only invited, unknown user or device, a remote user, a malformed id, a deactivated user) is skipped silently: the answer is identical to a delivered one. `*` expands at send time to the devices that exist then (a device created later does not receive it); for the sender's own user the sending device is excluded; an explicit device entry of the same user wins over `*` (one copy per device). A deactivated user receives nothing new, its queue is kept (reversible).
+
+*Transaction ids.* Scoped to (user, device, event type, txn id), persisted in `to_device_txns` and unique in the database, so a retry after a restart or on another node queues nothing again; the token is irrelevant (a new access token of the same device is the same transaction), another device, user, event type and the room `send` endpoint are independent transactions. A concurrent identical transaction is answered 429 at once (no waiting lock).
+
+*Acknowledgement on the NEXT token.* A sync response only delivers: its messages are deleted when a LATER sync presents the `since` token of that response (never on delivery). The to-device position travels in the token (`<core>.<position>[.<to-device position>]`, a device without traffic keeps its two-part token); a retry with an older token delivers the batch again, an initial sync, an unknown or garbage token and a token of another device (or user) acknowledge nothing and deliver everything pending. An acknowledgement covers exactly the batch a response carried: a message that committed late (a lower queue position than a delivered one) is neither deleted by it nor missed. `to_device` is always emitted, as `{"events": []}` when nothing is pending.
+
+*Wake-up.* A new message wakes the long poll of exactly its target device (a `{:to_device, device_id}` message to the `:pg` group of the user, cluster wide; the other devices of the user keep waiting); a message already queued that the presented token does not cover makes the sync return at once, one it covers does not.
+
+*Concurrency bounds.* A rate limit is not a concurrency limit: the rows of ONE request cost at most 1 MiB of fan-out (content + 128 per row; the targets beyond that are dropped silently like an unreachable one, the first target is always kept), so a 64 KiB content reaches at most 16 devices; at most 4 sends per user and 32 per node are in flight (`Nebu.Matrix.ToDeviceSendGate`, settings `to_device_max_concurrent_sends_per_user` and `to_device_max_concurrent_sends_per_node`; monitor based slots freed on `:DOWN`, node-local like the rate buckets), a send that finds no slot is answered 429 before its body is read.
+
+*Concurrency.* A request never waits for a lock while holding a pooled connection: the send takes the per-user `sync_global` advisory lock of the sender SHARED as a try-lock (the erasure takes it exclusively), the transaction has a short `lock_timeout`, a conflict is a 429; a send is a constant number of set-based statements however many recipients it names.
+
+*Lifecycle.* A device removal (`DELETE /devices`, `/delete_devices`, logout, logout-all, the Admin UI revoke, the OAuth logout) deletes the queue and the idempotency records of the device (`DeviceKeyStore.delete_device`, one place); revoking only the sessions of a device (the device and its keys stay) does not purge its queue, an admin deactivation keeps it (reversible), the messages a removed device SENT stay in the recipients' queues. The GDPR erasure deletes the queue and records of the user and the messages the user sent. The TTL job `Nebu.Workers.ToDevicePurgeWorker` is an Oban cron job (the leader enqueues, the purge is batched atomic SQL with `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` that concurrent runs split between them), no node-local state.
+
+*Client compatibility.* Verification and key requests work between your own devices and with any user you share a joined room with (an invitation does not count); a user you share no joined room with cannot be reached. Element and FluffyChat verify other users in the room, not through to-device messages, so the restriction costs them nothing.
+
 ### GDPR erasure inventory (story gdpr-erasure-global-data)
 
 GDPR erasure (`Nebu.Compliance.UserDeletion.delete_user_keys/3`) physically deletes every row of personal data that is stored under the user in a per-user table, in the SAME transaction, in this order: the `deletion_in_progress` marker, the private keys set to NULL, the row locks and the advisory lock (below), the per-user deletes (then a check that no `oauth_codes` row is left), the profile anonymisation, and `keys_deleted`; one failing step rolls everything back and writes the `user_keys_deletion_attempted` audit row, whose `error_detail` names the failing step (`step=<name>`, a code atom, never data). *Lock order:* the transaction first locks the user's `uia_sessions` rows and `cross_signing_keys` row (`FOR UPDATE`) and only THEN takes the per-user `sync_global` advisory lock of `AccountDataStore.put/3`, `CrossSigningKeyStore.put_keys/2` and the signature merge: the order of the cross-signing reset writer (uia row, cross-signing row, advisory lock), so a writer in flight is serialised with the erasure and the two do not deadlock (one narrow exception, below). Those three writers refuse a user whose `deletion_status` is set (`{:error, :user_erased}`, answered `403 M_USER_DEACTIVATED`). The key backup writers `RoomKeyBackupStore.create_version/3` and `RoomKeyBackupStore.put_sessions/3` take the same per-user `sync_global` lock and refuse an erased user the same way (they run after the lock, so a write in flight is serialised with the erasure; the lock is a try-lock, a busy lock is a 429, see "Server-side key backup"). The cross-signing writers (both branches of `device_signing/upload` and the signature merge) decide on the stored keys only through `CrossSigningKeyStore.lock_keys/1`, in the same order: the `cross_signing_keys` row lock, then the `sync_global` advisory lock, and only THEN the read (row -> advisory -> read; story cross-signing-upload-races), so two concurrent first uploads store one key set and a signature merge never writes back a stale merge base. The exception is the absent-then-present window (accepted residual): when the row lock of a signature merge found no row and a first upload commits the row before the merge gets the advisory lock, an erasure that locks the new row and waits for the advisory lock can form a cycle with the merge's write, which only now waits for that row; PostgreSQL aborts one side (`40P01`: the erasure fails closed, or the merge answers 500). A first upload has the same window when the row committed in between has a NULL master (a partial upload): its read sees no master and its upsert takes the row lock only after the advisory lock (pre-existing; the erasure fails closed, or the upload answers 500). Three actors of one user within milliseconds; closing it would need the advisory lock first everywhere, which the story rejected. Events authored by the user are retained (room history preservation, recital 65; immutable signed event log, the sender is the pseudonym); the profile is anonymised (`displayname = 'Deleted User'`, `avatar_url = NULL`, `users.anonymized_at`); admin deactivation keeps all of it (reversible). A second erasure answers `:conflict` and changes nothing.
 
 **The erasure is terminal for authentication.** The last step also sets `users.is_active = false` and NULLs the `users` PII columns (display name, avatar and email ciphertexts: the display name and avatar are encrypted with the server-wide key, so the key deletion does not shred them; also `last_seen_at` and `deactivation_reason`, admin free text that may describe the person). Every authentication gate (`TokenValidator`, `OAuthSessionStore.user_active/1`, `Nebu.Matrix.active?/1`, the account pages, `invitable_local_users`) treats `deletion_status IS NOT NULL` as deactivated, so neither a fresh IdP token, an SSO login, an OAuth authorization nor an admin reactivation revives the account, and `Profile.DB.upsert_profile/3` writes nothing for an erased user. A bearer (an unbound raw IdP JWT included) that the node-local provisioning cache of the OIDC plug (60 s TTL) already confirmed is still accepted on that node until the entry expires: new requests, not only one in flight, for up to 60 s, the same bound as for an admin deactivation. In that window it can still write the tables WITHOUT a guard (device keys, receipts, room account data, the lazy device row with its IP); the profile PUT, global account data, cross-signing, the signature merge and the sync persist are refused. The eviction of the cache entry on every node (a broadcast over `:pg` on erasure and deactivation) is the proper fix, recorded in follow-up `gn`. Admin UI access of an erased administrator follows the IdP admin group claim, not the erasure: remove the person from the IdP admin group. `Nebu.Admin.DB.set_is_active/2` refuses to reactivate an erased user (`:erased`, answered as a failed precondition) and the admin user suggestions never offer one.
 
-**Erased by the transaction** (`UserDeletion.erased_tables/0`, the list the deleting code and the guard test share): `user_room_account_data`, `account_data`, `cross_signing_keys`, `device_keys`, `device_one_time_keys`, `device_fallback_keys`, `forgotten_rooms`, `auto_join_opt_outs`, `read_receipts`, `uia_sessions`, `oauth_account_sessions`, `oauth_consent_requests`, `oauth_codes`, `room_key_backup_sessions`, `room_key_backup_versions` (the server-side key backup of story server-side-key-backup, migration 000058: every version row, the soft-delete markers included, and every session row). (`oauth_codes` needs the delete policy of migration 000055.)
+**Erased by the transaction** (`UserDeletion.erased_tables/0`, the list the deleting code and the guard test share): `user_room_account_data`, `account_data`, `cross_signing_keys`, `device_keys`, `device_one_time_keys`, `device_fallback_keys`, `forgotten_rooms`, `auto_join_opt_outs`, `read_receipts`, `uia_sessions`, `oauth_account_sessions`, `oauth_consent_requests`, `oauth_codes`, `room_key_backup_sessions`, `room_key_backup_versions` (the server-side key backup of story server-side-key-backup, migration 000058: every version row, the soft-delete markers included, and every session row). `to_device_messages`, `to_device_txns` (the to-device queue and its idempotency records of story to-device-messaging, migration 000059: every message queued FOR the user, every record, and, by one more statement on `sender`, every message the user SENT that waits in another user's queue). `ToDeviceStore.send` is a `sync_global` user like the key backup writers: it takes the user's lock SHARED as a try-lock (a busy lock is a 429 for the sender and a silent skip for a recipient) and refuses an erased sender with `403 M_USER_DEACTIVATED`, so a send in flight never re-creates rows after the erasure. (`oauth_codes` needs the delete policy of migration 000055.)
 
 **Ended after the commit by the session teardown** (best effort, outside the transaction): `devices`, `matrix_sessions`, `jwt_device_bindings`, `sessions`, `oauth_idp_refresh_leases`, `sync_tokens`, `sync_room_cursors` (the dispatcher also removes the `devices` rows of legacy devices).
 
